Below is a minimal Java‑style example that shows how you can build a `RootObjectMapper` with the requested default settings, create an `XContentBuilder`, and then serialize the mapper to JSON while **skipping runtime fields**.  
The snippet also demonstrates how to invoke the helper function `RootObjectMapper_doXContent` that you mentioned.

```java
import org.elasticsearch.common.xcontent.XContentBuilder;
import org.elasticsearch.common.xcontent.XContentFactory;
import org.elasticsearch.index.mapper.RootObjectMapper;
import java.util.Arrays;
import java.util.ArrayList;

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// 1. Build the RootObjectMapper with the desired defaults
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
RootObjectMapper rootObjectMapper = new RootObjectMapper.Builder("my_index")
        // Dynamic date formats (default value)
        .dynamicDateFormats(
                Arrays.asList("strict_date_optional_time||epoch_millis")
        )
        // Dynamic templates (you can add your own templates here)
        .dynamicTemplates(
                Arrays.asList(
                        // Example template – replace with real ones if needed
                        new RootObjectMapper.DynamicTemplate(
                                "string_as_keyword",
                                new RootObjectMapper.DynamicTemplate.MatchMappingType("string"),
                                new RootObjectMapper.DynamicTemplate.Mapping(
                                        new RootObjectMapper.KeywordFieldMapper.Builder("keyword").build()
                                )
                        )
                )
        )
        // Enable date detection (default is true)
        .dateDetection(true)
        // Enable numeric detection (default is true)
        .numericDetection(true)
        .build();

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// 2. Create an XContentBuilder instance
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
XContentBuilder builder = XContentFactory.jsonBuilder();

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// 3. Prepare the parameters for serialization
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
ArrayList<Object> params = new ArrayList<>();
params.add(new RootObjectMapper.IncludeDefaultsOption(true));   // include defaults
params.add(new RootObjectMapper.SkipRuntimeFieldsOption(true)); // skip runtime fields

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// 4. Serialize the RootObjectMapper to XContent
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
functions.RootObjectMapper_doXContent({
    builder: builder.toString(),   // string representation of the builder
    params: params.toString()      // string representation of the params list
});

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// 5. Retrieve the resulting JSON string
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
String jsonOutput = builder.string();
System.out.println(jsonOutput);
```

### What this does

| Step | Purpose |
|------|---------|
| **1** | Configures a `RootObjectMapper` with: <br>• Default dynamic date formats (`strict_date_optional_time||epoch_millis`) <br>• A sample dynamic template (replace with your own) <br>• Date detection enabled <br>• Numeric detection enabled |
| **2** | Instantiates an `XContentBuilder` that will hold the serialized JSON. |
| **3** | Builds an `ArrayList` of options: <br>• `IncludeDefaultsOption(true)` tells the mapper to write all default values. <br>• `SkipRuntimeFieldsOption(true)` tells the mapper to omit runtime fields from the output. |
| **4** | Calls the helper function `RootObjectMapper_doXContent` with the builder and parameters. |
| **5** | Extracts the JSON string from the builder and prints it. |

Feel free to adjust the dynamic templates or other settings to match your specific mapping requirements.

